---
import { mastheadStamp } from "../../dossiers/headline.js";
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';import EveryCut from '../../components/EveryCut.astro';import GuideData from '../../components/GuideData.astro';import {bySlug,kicker} from '../../dossiers/registry.js';import {energyModel} from '../../dossiers/energy.model.js';
import {encodeEnergyPayload} from '../../dossiers/energy.payload.js';
const D=bySlug.energy,model=energyModel(),groups=[...new Set(model.plates.map(p=>p.group))],payload=encodeEnergyPayload(model);
const F=model.facts,fy=y=>`${y}-${String(+y+1).slice(2)}`;
---
<Base title="Energy flows and electricity · India Atlas" description={D.description} nav="guides" dateStamp={mastheadStamp(D)} crumbLeft='<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Environment / <b>Energy</b>' crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`} canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/energy/" ogImage="/og/energy.png">
<div class="page energy-page">
 <div class="titleblock break" data-inputs="energy-report-2026-balance-ktoe, energy-ember-national-electricity-demand-twh, energy-ember-national-carbon-intensity-g_co2e_per_kwh" data-prose="evergreen and data-bound facts"><div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div><div class="hero-grid"><h1 set:html={D.headline}/><p class="lede">Coal still does most of the work in India's energy system: it fires the power stations, the steel plants and the cement kilns. Oil moves the trucks, buses and scooters, and most of it is imported. Solar and wind are growing fast from a small base.</p></div><div class="meta"><span>Energy accounts <b>{fy(F.supply.period)}</b></span><span>Electricity <b>{F.demand.period}</b></span><span>Sources <b>MoSPI Energy Statistics India 2026; Ember</b></span></div>
  <div class="numbers"><div class="num"><div class="l">Primary energy supply</div><div class="v acc" id="n-supply">–</div><div class="d">All fuels entering the economy, {fy(F.supply.period)}</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Energy reaching users</div><div class="v" id="n-final">–</div><div class="d">After power stations, refineries and losses</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Electricity used in a year</div><div class="v" id="n-demand">–</div><div class="d">Calendar {F.demand.period}, Ember estimate</div></div><div class="num"><div class="l">Carbon per unit of electricity</div><div class="v" id="n-intensity">–</div><div class="d">Grams of CO₂ equivalent per kWh</div></div></div>
 </div>
 <section data-inputs="energy-report-2026-balance-ktoe, energy-report-2026-petroleum-ktoe" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>From a kitchen flame to a factory furnace</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>A family meets energy as a cooking-gas cylinder, a fill of petrol for the scooter and a monthly electricity bill. Behind each sits a long chain: a coal mine in Jharkhand, a tanker unloading crude at Jamnagar, a power station that turns only part of the coal's energy into electricity and loses the rest as heat.</p><p>The government's energy accounts follow that chain from mines, wells and imports to the people and firms who finally use the energy. They show how much of the country's energy is bought abroad, how much is lost along the way and which parts of the economy use the most.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>Electricity gets its own section because it is where the change is happening. Coal plants still produce most of it, while solar and wind grow fastest. Ember, an independent energy think tank, estimates generation, demand and emissions year by year and state by state.</p><p>The accounts measure quantities, not prices. They cannot say what a family paid, how often the power went off or whether a household could afford enough cooking fuel.</p></div></div></section>
 <section data-inputs="all energy bundles" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>How to read these accounts</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="wm-grid"><div><p><b>One unit for every fuel.</b> Coal is mined in tonnes, gas in cubic metres and electricity in kilowatt-hours. To add them, the accounts convert each into the energy a tonne of oil would give: a million tonnes of oil equivalent (Mtoe) is the energy in a million tonnes of crude. The ministry publishes thousands of tonnes; this guide shows millions.</p><p><b>Supply comes before use.</b> Primary supply is the energy entering the economy, from domestic mines and wells and from imports, less exports. Energy reaching users is what is left after power stations, refineries, the energy industry's own use and transmission losses. The gap between them is mostly heat lost in turning coal into electricity.</p><p><b>Financial years and calendar years.</b> The ministry's accounts run from April to March; Ember's electricity figures run from January to December.</p><p><b>Emissions are estimates.</b> Ember estimates the greenhouse gases released in making electricity, counting each fuel over its life cycle and converting all gases into their carbon dioxide equivalent (CO₂e). They are not measurements at a chimney.</p></div><div class="wm-rail"><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">Plants, not people</span>State figures follow where power plants stand. Electricity flows between states, so a coal state's mix is not what its homes use.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">Two editions</span>The 2026 report revised earlier years. Older editions, kept in the tables below, are not mixed into its series.</aside></div></div></section>
 <section id="current-reading" data-inputs="energy-report-2026-balance-ktoe, energy-ember-national-electricity-generation-twh, energy-ember-national-electricity-generation-pct, energy-ember-national-electricity-demand-mwh_per_person, energy-ember-national-power-sector-emissions-mt_co2e" data-prose="data-bound facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>What the accounts show</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>India's primary energy supply rose from <b id="n-supply_first">–</b> in {fy(F.supply_first.period)} to the latest figure above, an increase of <b id="n-supply_growth">–</b>. Coal provides <b id="n-coal_supply_share">–</b> of it. Imports cover <b id="n-crude_import_share">–</b> of the crude oil the country refines.</p><p>Only <b id="n-final_share">–</b> of the energy supplied reaches users, mostly because power stations turn much of their fuel into waste heat. Industry takes <b id="n-industry_share">–</b> of what does reach users.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>Coal generated <b id="n-coal_gen_share">–</b> of India's electricity in {F.coal_gen_share.period}. Clean sources, which include solar, wind, hydro, bioenergy and nuclear, supplied <b id="n-clean_share">–</b>, up from <b id="n-clean_share_2015">–</b> in {F.clean_share_2015.period}. Solar alone went from <b id="n-solar_twh_2015">–</b> to <b id="n-solar_twh">–</b> over those years.</p><p>Electricity use per person has risen from <b id="n-demand_pp_2000">–</b> a year in {F.demand_pp_2000.period} to <b id="n-demand_pp">–</b>. Generating it released an estimated <b id="n-emissions">–</b> of greenhouse gases in {F.emissions.period}.</p></div></div></section>
 {groups.map(group=><section data-inputs={[...new Set(model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).flatMap(p=>p.sourceBundles))].join(', ')} data-prose="evergreen explanations and data-bound facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>{group}</h2><span class="line"></span></div>{model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=><div class="break"><figure id={'cb-'+p.id} data-inputs={p.sourceBundles.join(', ')}><div class="plate-head"><div class="t"><h3>{p.title}</h3><div class="sub">{p.sub}</div></div><div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id={'pv-'+p.id}>–</div><div class="c">{p.kind==='history'?'Latest year':p.rows[0].name}</div></div></div><div class="frame"><div class="plotzone"><div id={'plot-'+p.id}></div></div><div class="plotfoot"><span class="src">{p.source}, {p.period}.</span><span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href={p.sourceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span></div></div><div class="explain"><div class="ev"><span class="mk">How to read this</span><p>{p.explain}</p></div></div></figure></div>)}</section>)}
 <section data-inputs="all energy bundles" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Common misreadings</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p><b>"More solar capacity means that much more electricity."</b> Capacity is what a plant can produce at full tilt. A solar panel works only in daylight and a wind turbine only in wind, so they generate far less over a year than their rating suggests.</p><p><b>"Clean and renewable mean the same thing."</b> In Ember's figures, clean includes nuclear and renewable does not.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p><b>"The gap between supply and use is waste."</b> Most of it is heat lost in power stations, which is unavoidable with today's coal plants. Some is the energy industry's own use, and a small part is an accounting difference.</p><p><b>"A state's generation mix is its residents' electricity."</b> The figures follow where plants stand. A city like Delhi draws much of its power from plants in other states.</p></div></div></section>
 <EveryCut slug="energy"/><GuideData slug="energy"/>
 <section data-inputs="all energy bundles" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Sources</h2><span class="line"></span></div><p>The energy accounts are MoSPI's Energy Statistics India 2026, from its original Excel tables, with earlier editions kept apart in the tables above. Electricity generation, demand and emissions are Ember's national and state releases. Each chart above answers one question; every published line, fuel and state is in the table above and the downloads.</p><p><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in/themes/product/60-energy-statistics">MoSPI: Energy Statistics India</a>. <a href="https://ember-energy.org/data/">Ember: electricity data and methodology</a>.</p></section>
</div><script is:inline type="application/json" id="energy-data" set:html={JSON.stringify(payload).replace(/</g,'<')}/><script>import '../../dossiers/energy.client.js';import '../../dossiers/guide.client.js';</script>
<style>.energy-page :global(.em-cite p){overflow-wrap:anywhere}.energy-page :global(.numbers .v){font-size:clamp(24px,3.3vw,42px)}.energy-page :global(.plate-val .v){font-size:clamp(22px,2.8vw,34px)}.energy-page :global(.plotzone){overflow:visible}@media(max-width:680px){.energy-page :global(.plate-head){display:block}.energy-page :global(.plate-val){text-align:left;margin-top:12px}.energy-page :global(.plate-head h3){max-width:100%}.energy-page :global(.plotzone > [id^="plot-"] > svg){min-width:0;max-width:100%;height:auto}}</style>
</Base>
